🚀 Argo CD + Minikube Learning Notes
1. Accessing Argo CD UI
NodePort access:

Example: http://192.168.49.2:31466 (Minikube VM IP + NodePort).

May fail if host cannot route into Minikube VM network.

Tunnel access (recommended):

Run minikube tunnel.

Access via http://127.0.0.1:<random-port> (localhost).

More reliable for local dev.

2. Installing Argo CD CLI in WSL
Check architecture:

bash
uname -m   # usually x86_64 → use amd64 binary
Download CLI:

bash
curl -L -o argocd https://github.com/argoproj/argo-cd/releases/latest/download/argocd-linux-amd64
chmod +x argocd
sudo mv argocd /usr/local/bin/argocd
Verify:

bash
argocd version
3. Logging in via CLI
Use tunnel port:

bash
argocd login 127.0.0.1:45401
Username: admin

Password:

bash
kubectl -n argocd get secret argocd-initial-admin-secret \
  -o jsonpath="{.data.password}" | base64 -d
4. Creating an Application from GitHub Repo
Example: Guestbook app from repo nandini867/argocd-example-apps

bash
argocd app create guestbook \
  --repo https://github.com/nandini867/argocd-example-apps.git \
  --path guestbook \
  --dest-server https://kubernetes.default.svc \
  --dest-namespace default
Sync manually:

bash
argocd app sync guestbook
Check status:

bash
argocd app get guestbook
5. Enabling Automatic Sync
Enable auto‑sync:

bash
argocd app set guestbook --sync-policy automated
Optional flags:

--self-heal → auto‑correct drift if cluster changes.

--auto-prune → delete resources removed from Git.

Example:

bash
argocd app set guestbook --sync-policy automated --self-heal --auto-prune
6. Key Takeaways
NodePort vs Tunnel: Tunnel is easier for local browser access.

CLI install: Always use curl -L to follow redirects.

Name collisions: Keep CLI binary separate from server pods.

GitOps workflow: Repo + path → Argo CD app → sync → auto‑sync.
